            <ArticleTitle>Sustainable Model for Public Health Emergency Operations Centers for Global Settings.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3201/eid2313.170435</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Capacity to receive, verify, analyze, assess, and investigate public health events is essential for epidemic intelligence. Public health Emergency Operations Centers (PHEOCs) can be epidemic intelligence hubs by 1) having the capacity to receive, analyze, and visualize multiple data streams, including surveillance and 2) maintaining a trained workforce that can analyze and interpret data from real-time emerging events. Such PHEOCs could be physically located within a ministry of health epidemiology, surveillance, or equivalent department rather than exist as a stand-alone space and serve as operational hubs during nonoutbreak times but in emergencies can scale up according to the traditional Incident Command System structure.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
